      "id": 1,
      "text": "Aurora was laid out in 1871 by David Stone, who named it after his hometown of Aurora, Illinois.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurora%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1876 the county seat shifted from the extinct town of Orville City to Aurora, cementing its regional importance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurora%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Hamilton County Courthouse, standing in Aurora’s historic downtown, earned a spot on the National Register of Historic Places in 1985.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://auroranebraska.com/Featured/what-to-do-in-aurora",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Plainsman Museum in Aurora’s showcases a one‑room schoolhouse, a blacksmith shop, and Civil War hero General Delevan’s home, preserving local heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://auroranebraska.com/Featured/what-to-do-in-aurora",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Aurora’s Central Park Square, informally called “The Square,” features a preserved courthouse, inviting lawn, and historic shops that draw visitors.",
